# Day 18

**27km / 9.5 hours**

I have grumbled quite a bit over the previous days but this day was incredible, the best so far. It began late since I was planning to wait for the tide to recede before wading through the estuary but extremely fortunately a family was around and took pity on me, allowing me to kayak to the end of the estuary which saved me some valuable extra time. 

![The somewhat deep water I would've had to have wasted through since it wasn't yet low tide](./estuary.jpg)

After that was some nice road walking up a hill towards a nice little Kauri mountain track which has some great forests and views of the ocean and of the beach ahead. 

![The lovely trees on the path in the forest](./forest.jpg)

![The view of the beach ahead](./beach-scenery.jpg)

Then I got down to the beach and walked on the beach for a while. At the beginning this was euphoric as it was a beautiful afternoon and a fantastic beach but it m as it dragged on and on it became less appealing. It began to remind me of the start of the trail which featured multiple days of just walking on the beach. At the final stretch of the beach it connected near the road so there were plenty of people playing around there.

![The view from the beach, what a great day for it](./beach-view.jpg)

Then the track went up onto the hill/ridge and into the woods/rocks where you could see the ocean and the forests. There was also some historical artefacts around but I was running out of time so skipped over them somewhat. The changes in elevation did make this section a bit tough, especially after the previous bouts but it wasn't too arduous.

![A nice gap in the rocks to pass through along the path](./rock-gap.jpg)

![The view of the beautiful ocean and coastline](./ocean-view.jpg)

After that was done small amount of road walking but nothing excessive.
This was probably my favourite day so far since it was varied and featured some stunning views. Truly some of the experiences that make you happy to be alive.